Practical No. 19
OAuth2 Login with Google and Accessing a Secured REST API
Spring Boot 3 + Maven + H2 Database + Spring Data JPA + OAuth2 Client
Objective
To implement the OAuth2 Authorization Code grant with Google using Spring Security's OAuth2 Client
support, log in through the browser, and call secured REST endpoints (profile and full CRUD on per-user
Notes stored in H2) with the token obtained through Postman's OAuth 2.0 authorization feature.
Design note (why this project also has a JWT decoder): oauth2Login on its own creates a browser session
and does not accept Bearer tokens. Your Postman steps 7 and 8 send a token, so the same application is also
configured as a resource server. Google's access token is opaque and cannot be verified locally, but Google's
ID token is a signed JWT. Therefore Postman must send the ID token (Postman option: Use Token Type -> ID
Token), and the API verifies its signature, issuer and audience (must equal your Client ID).
How it works
 Browser flow (steps 5-6) Postman flow (steps 7-8)
 ------------------------ ------------------------
 /oauth2/authorization/google Get New Access Token (Authorization Code)
 | |
 v v
 Google login + consent --code--> Spring Boot Google login + consent --code--> Postman
 | (/login/oauth2/code/google) | Postman calls the Token URL and
 v v receives access_token + id_token
 Session cookie (JSESSIONID) GET /api/user/profile
 -> /api/user/profile Authorization: Bearer <ID token (JWT)>
 |
 Spring Boot verifies the JWT with Google's
 public keys, then runs the controller
