Key Points to Remember
• Two authentication styles in one app: browser = oauth2Login + session cookie; API client = Bearer
ID token verified as a JWT. Both end in the same controllers through the Authentication object.
• @AuthenticationPrincipal OAuth2User (as in your class diagram) works only for the browser session.
For a Bearer call the principal is a Jwt, so the controller reads the generic Authentication instead.
• Authorization Code flow: the user logs in at Google (the app never sees the password); Google
returns a one-time code to the redirect URI; the client exchanges it (with the client secret) at the Token
URL for tokens.
Practical No. 19 - OAuth2 Login with Google and Secured REST API Page 8
• redirect_uri_mismatch error means the URI sent by the client is not listed exactly in Google Console
(check http vs https, port, path).
• Access denied / app not verified: add your account under Test users on the OAuth consent screen.
• Audience check: without it, an ID token Google issued to a different application would be accepted
here.
• GitHub instead of Google: register the app under GitHub Developer Settings (redirect
http://localhost:8080/login/oauth2/code/github) and use the registration name github.
GitHub has no ID token, so its opaque access token could not be checked with a JWT decoder; the API
would need a custom OpaqueTokenIntrospector that calls https://api.github.com/user.
• Never commit the client secret; use environment variables, and use HTTPS outside local testing.
Conclusion
The application implements the OAuth2 Authorization Code flow with Google for browser login and also
verifies Google-issued ID tokens sent as Bearer tokens. An authenticated user can read their profile and
create, update and delete their own Notes in H2, while missing, expired or altered tokens are rejected with
401.
